  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "CaP5BYfspuZOHMfeErsBY/Sjlyw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:03.135Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Incentro passes the criteria that usually matter most in this corpus: good open-source posture, the right size, and a Camunda practice that sits exactly where an append-only decision record attaches. It was dropped anyway, on the newer lens, because it is a Google Cloud partner serving customers from finance through to retail \u2014 cloud-anchored and commercially focused, which the assessment names as the opposite of local-first sovereign deployment.\n\nThe mechanism is about where a partner's architecture defaults live rather than which licence its tools carry. A firm whose delivery pattern assumes a hyperscaler will reach for managed services first; asking it to lead with client-owned infrastructure inverts the reference architecture it sells and the cloud-partner incentives attached to it. Open-source tooling on top of a hyperscaler is still not sovereign deployment.\n\nThe entry is explicitly labelled a clean example of the thesis doing real filtering work, and that is the reusable point. A screen that only ever confirms leads the previous criteria already liked is not filtering; this one removed a lead that scored well on size and posture, which is evidence the sovereignty criterion is load-bearing rather than decorative.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Incentro (NL \u00b7 mid)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DROPPED on the new lens. Good OSS posture and right size, but it is a GOOGLE CLOUD partner serving finance-to-retail - cloud-anchored and commercially focused, which is the opposite of local-first sovereign deployment. A clean example of the thesis doing real filtering work.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:26:03.135480Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:03.135485Z"
    }
  },
